% Port of interactive-lp/examples/cellular-automata.cep — and the
% original is a SKELETON: its header reads "2022-02-16 / Set up
% predicates but wrote no actual 'code' yet." Both stages are empty,
% the context is empty, and the trace starts and ends at once. The
% port is one-to-one with that: the grid, the neighbour relation,
% the two (empty) stages — no update rule is invented here. Writing
% the automaton's rules (one generation = one compute/main lap, the
% synchronous update carried by stage quiescence) is left as the
% hands-on next step, a v2 for the user to author.
%
% Encodings (recipe rows "finite constructor terms", "Ceptre
% builtins"):
%   - `nat` with `s` and `#builtin NAT/NAT_ZERO/NAT_SUCC`: the
%     builtin directives have no LLP counterpart (metis types are
%     finite, enumerated). nat is bounded to a 3x3 grid, n0..n2, and
%     `s` becomes the bwd successor graph s(X,SX) — the bound is the
%     price of the finite-type fragment, stated here.
%   - `pos nat nat : cell` (a constructor): cell constants c<x><y>
%     plus the bwd graph pos(C,X,Y), as in tower_of_hanoi.
%   - the original's eight `nbr` facts with (s X) patterns become
%     eight Horn rules over pos + s, same directions, same order.
%     (They are the original's literally: e.g. nbr (pos X Y) (pos
%     (s X) Y) and its converse are both listed, so nbr is
%     symmetric — but only the diagonal (s X)(s Y) -> X Y and its
%     converse appear, not the anti-diagonal: a cell sees 6 of its 8
%     Moore neighbours. Kept, not corrected.)
%   - the stage order puts `main` first: the original's trace is
%     `#trace _ main init`, and a metis run starts in the first
%     declared stage.
% No rules, so no $-overlap sites and no pumping rules.
catalog cellular-automata 1.
provenance hand "port:interactive-lp/examples/cellular-automata.cep" "2026-10-03".
layers (story).
stages (main compute_neighbor_values).
type nat {n0 n1 n2}.
type cell {c00 c01 c02 c10 c11 c12 c20 c21 c22}.
bwd s(nat,nat).
bwd pos(cell,nat,nat).
bwd nbr(cell,cell).
s(n0,n1).
s(n1,n2).
pos(c00,n0,n0).
pos(c01,n0,n1).
pos(c02,n0,n2).
pos(c10,n1,n0).
pos(c11,n1,n1).
pos(c12,n1,n2).
pos(c20,n2,n0).
pos(c21,n2,n1).
pos(c22,n2,n2).
% the original's eight nbr facts, in order:
nbr(C,D) :- pos(C,SX,SY), pos(D,X,SY), s(X,SX), s(Y,SY).
nbr(C,D) :- pos(C,SX,SY), pos(D,SX,Y), s(X,SX), s(Y,SY).
nbr(C,D) :- pos(C,SX,SY), pos(D,X,Y), s(X,SX), s(Y,SY).
nbr(C,D) :- pos(C,X,SY), pos(D,X,Y), s(Y,SY).
nbr(C,D) :- pos(C,SX,Y), pos(D,X,Y), s(X,SX).
nbr(C,D) :- pos(C,X,Y), pos(D,SX,Y), s(X,SX).
nbr(C,D) :- pos(C,X,Y), pos(D,X,SY), s(Y,SY).
nbr(C,D) :- pos(C,X,Y), pos(D,SX,SY), s(X,SX), s(Y,SY).
stage compute_neighbor_values {
}
stage main {
}
% context init = {} and `#trace _ main init.`:
#steps 1
#seeds 1
